# Findings that cannot fail the platform image scan, and why (T-6.10, #332).
#
# HOW THIS FILE IS USED, WHICH IS NARROWER THAN THE FILENAME SUGGESTS
#
# It is passed ONLY to the gating step of security.yml's `platform image` job.
# The reporting step does not read it, so everything below still reaches the
# Security tab and is still counted in the run's output. Nothing here disappears
# — it stops failing a build, which is a smaller thing than being ignored.
#
# It is NOT applied to the `image` job. Findings in core and gateway are ours to
# fix: we choose the base image and we rebuild on demand.
#
# THE RULE FOR ADDING ONE
#
# Trivy's `ignore-unfixed` filters on whether a fixed PACKAGE exists. For an
# image this repository does not build, the question that decides whether anyone
# here can act is whether a fixed IMAGE exists, and those are not the same. The
# gap between them belongs to the image publisher, and a gate that fires inside
# it is red for something nobody here can resolve — which security.yml's own
# header explains is how a check stops being read.
#
# So an entry is justified only when BOTH hold:
#
#   1. no rebuilt image exists to repin to — checked, not assumed
#   2. the finding is recorded here with the version that would clear it
#
# If a rebuilt image exists, the answer is to repin. That is what happened to
# valkey in this same change: its previous digest carried 3 CRITICALs and 27
# HIGHs, a newer build existed, and repinning was the fix rather than an entry
# in this file.
#
# EVERY ENTRY IS PROVISIONAL
#
# There is no expiry mechanism here, so #332 stays open as the thing that makes
# someone look again. An entry with no card behind it is how a documented
# exception becomes a permanent one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-33630 — c-ares, use-after-free / double-free. HIGH.
#
# curlimages/curl ships c-ares 1.34.6-r0; the fix is 1.34.8-r0.
#
# Verified on 2026-08-28: `curlimages/curl:latest` resolves to
# sha256:7c12af72…, which is byte for byte the digest pinned in core.yaml and
# gateway.yaml, and still carries 1.34.6-r0. There is no newer image. Repinning
# is not available as a response.
#
# Reachability, for whoever revisits this: the initContainer makes exactly one
# HTTPS GET to the cluster's own OIDC issuer, in a loop, and exits. It resolves
# one hostname the platform controls. That is not an argument that the finding
# does not matter — it is the context for deciding how hard to push on it.
CVE-2026-33630

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-14456 — OpenSSL, denial of service via unbounded allocation. HIGH.
#
# Affects both digest-pinned images. The fix is libcrypto3 3.5.8-r0 and the
# newest build of either image carries 3.5.7-r0:
#
#   curlimages/curl   sha256:7c12af72…   3.5.7-r0
#   valkey/valkey     sha256:e0eb7c48…   3.5.7-r0   (already the newer build)
#
# So this one survives the valkey repin rather than being fixed by it, which is
# the clearest possible demonstration that "a fix exists" and "a fix is
# available to us" are different questions.
CVE-2026-14456
